.TH mkproto "" "Administration"
.SH NAME
mkproto \- insert bad blocks into a proto specification
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fB/etc/mkproto \fIdevice \fB< \fIproto1 \fB> \fIproto2\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
mkproto - insert bad blocks into a proto specification
Usage: /etc/mkproto device < proto1 >proto2
Standard input should specify all parts of the desired proto file except
bad block specifications which mkproto will insert after scanning the
specified device for unreadable blocks.
If the root directory specification is missing, mkproto will append one
.HE
.B mkproto
builds a complete
.I proto
description file, of the kind read by
.BR mkfs ,
by scanning
.I device
for bad blocks and inserting the resulting
.B %b
bad-block lines into an otherwise complete proto file read from the
standard input.
.PP
The standard input must already contain the boot/name/pack line and
the block-count line of the proto file (see
.BR mkfs );
.B mkproto
copies these two lines through unchanged to the standard output.
It then opens
.I device
and reads it one block at a time, from block 0 up to the block count
just read; for each block that cannot be read, it emits the block
number in a
.B %b
line, exactly as if the bad block had been listed by hand in a proto
file.
.PP
After the bad-block lines,
.B mkproto
copies the rest of the standard input (the directory and file
specifications) through to the standard output unchanged.
If the standard input is exhausted immediately after the block-count
line, that is, it specifies no root directory at all,
.B mkproto
appends a minimal root directory specification of its own:
.DS
d\-\-755 3 1
$
.DE
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.IP "Cannot open %s"
The named
.I device
could not be opened for reading.
.IP "Unexpected end of file on proto input"
The standard input ended before
.B mkproto
had read both the boot/name/pack line and the block-count line.
.IP "Preposterous block size: %D"
The block count given on the standard input is less than four.
.IP "Bad super block"
Block 1, where the super block must live, cannot be read.
.IP "Bad root inode block"
Block 2, the first i-node block, cannot be read.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B mkfs
.SH NOTES
.B mkproto
reads
.I device
sequentially from block 0 and does not rewind it; run it against the
device to be scanned before that device is used to hold a live file
system.
